## Magnesium — Evidence Summary

According to the NHS, magnesium is a mineral that helps turn the food we eat into energy and helps ensure the parathyroid glands, which produce hormones important for bone health, work normally. The NHS also describes magnesium as a metallic element mainly found in the bones and essential to life, and notes that it is one of the electrolytes — minerals in the blood that carry a tiny electric charge.

The NHS states that magnesium is found in a wide variety of foods, including spinach, nuts, and wholemeal bread.

The NHS gives the UK reference intake as 300 mg per day for men aged 19 to 64, and 270 mg per day for women aged 19 to 64. Regarding supplementation, the NHS states that taking more than 400 mg per day for a short time can cause diarrhoea, and that there is not enough evidence to say what the effects of taking high doses over a long time might be. The NHS also states that having 400 mg or less per day from supplements is unlikely to cause any harm.

On the question of specific magnesium preparations, NICE guidance on preventing recurrent hypomagnesaemia notes that oral magnesium glycerophosphate has marketing authorisation in the UK for that clinical purpose, and that no other oral magnesium supplements hold that authorisation for the same indication. The BNF, via NICE, lists magnesium citrate as a named preparation within the prescribing framework.

