**Vitamin D** helps regulate the amount of calcium and phosphate in the body; the NHS states these nutrients are needed to keep bones, teeth, and muscles healthy (NHS: https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-d). Food sources identified by the NHS include oily fish (such as salmon, sardines, herring, trout, and mackerel), red meat, liver, egg yolks, and certain fortified foods such as some fat spreads and breakfast cereals; the NHS also notes that cows' milk in the UK is generally not a good source, as it is not fortified as in some other countries. The NHS and NICE both state that the reference nutrient intake for adults and children aged four and over is 10 micrograms (µg) per day, equivalent to 400 IU per day. Regarding the upper limit, the NHS states: "Do not take more than 100 micrograms (4,000 IU) of vitamin D a day as it could be harmful," and separately states that taking too many vitamin D supplements over a long period of time can cause too much calcium to build up in the body (hypercalcaemia). A NICE expert paper additionally notes that in Europe and the USA, safe upper limits have been set at 50 µg/day (2,000 IU) for adults, though it acknowledges these figures are described as controversial in that document. The NICE expert paper also notes that vitamin D is present in licensed medicines as well as dietary supplements.

