---

**Vitamin B12 — Evidence Summary**

The NHS states that vitamin B12 performs several important functions in the body, including keeping the nervous system healthy (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamin-b12-or-folate-deficiency-anaemia). NHS 111 Wales further notes that vitamin B12 combines with a protein called intrinsic factor in the stomach, and that this combination is then absorbed in part of the gut (https://111.wales.nhs.uk/encyclopaedia/a/article/anaemia,vitaminb12andfolatedeficiency).

According to the NHS, good dietary sources of vitamin B12 include meat, salmon and cod, milk and other dairy products, and eggs (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamin-b12-or-folate-deficiency-anaemia/treatment). A NICE draft guideline notes that vegetarian and vegan foods fortified with vitamin B12 are now available for those who do not eat, or limit their intake of, animal-sourced foods (https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng239/documents/draft-guideline).

No UK reference intake figure or published upper limit for Vitamin B12 was traceable to the NHS, NICE, or BNF sources retrieved.

Regarding regulatory status, the NHS states that hydroxocobalamin — the injectable form of Vitamin B12 used in the UK — is available on prescription only (https://www.nhs.uk/medicines/hydroxocobalamin/about-hydroxocobalamin). The NHS also states that hydroxocobalamin is preferred to cyanocobalamin for UK injections because it remains in the body for longer (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamin-b12-or-folate-deficiency-anaemia/treatment). A NICE technology overview describes current UK clinical practice as offering intramuscular injections of hydroxocobalamin when a diagnosis of vitamin B12 deficiency is confirmed (https://www.nice.org.uk/advice/mib40/chapter/technology-overview).

