**Regarding your question — "Is melatonin available over the counter in the UK?"**

I record and cite published sources rather than advise. The relevant sourced facts are below, included within the full evidence summary.

---

**Melatonin: Evidence Summary**

The NHS describes melatonin as a natural hormone produced by the pineal gland that helps control the sleep cycle. According to the NHS, the body produces melatonin after it gets dark, with levels peaking in the early hours of the morning and reducing during daylight hours, acting on receptors in the body to encourage sleep (https://www.nhs.uk/medicines/melatonin/common-questions-about-melatonin). NICE similarly states that melatonin is a hormone occurring naturally in the body that is involved in regulating sleep and circadian rhythms (https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/es38).

On the specific question of over-the-counter availability: the NHS states that melatonin is available on prescription only in the UK, and that while melatonin supplements may be available to buy in health food shops or online in some countries, such supplements are not authorised for sale in the UK (https://www.nhs.uk/medicines/melatonin/about-melatonin; https://www.nhs.uk/medicines/melatonin/common-questions-about-melatonin). No UK authority source identified a reference intake or dietary food sources for melatonin, as it is not classified as a dietary nutrient with a population reference value in any retrieved UK source.

